/-
# A soluble variety not generated by its finite groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 5.43)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 5.43 (A. Yu. Olshanskii): does there exist a
soluble variety of groups that is not generated by its finite groups?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Solvable
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.GroupLawsShared

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.SolubleVarietyNotFinitelyGeneratedKourovka5Problem43Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 5.43 (A. Yu.
Olshanskii). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700325 (`KOU-5.43`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Laws and varieties are `SatisfiesLaws` of the Kourovka 11.21 entry, reused here, and consequence is
that of the shared module `WantedExt.GroupTheory.GroupLawsShared`: a law is a word of `FreeGroup ℕ`,
the variety defined by a set `W` of laws consists of the groups in which every law of `W` holds,
every variety arises this way, and consequence is semantic over all groups in `Type` (equivalently,
by the completeness theorem, derivability). A variety is soluble when all of its groups have derived
length at most some `d` (a variety consisting of soluble groups has bounded derived length, its free
group of countable rank being soluble). It is generated by its finite groups exactly when every law
holding in all of its finite groups is a consequence of its defining laws.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FormationVarietyPGroupsKourovka11Problem21Wanted (SatisfiesLaws)
open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.GroupLawsShared

/-- Kourovka 5.43 (A. Yu. Olshanskii): there is a soluble variety of groups (all of its groups have
derived length at most some `d`) that is not generated by its finite groups: some law holds in all
finite groups of the variety but is not a consequence of its defining laws. -/
def existsSolubleVarietyNotGeneratedByFinite : Prop :=
  ∃ W : Set (FreeGroup ℕ),
    (∃ d : ℕ, ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], SatisfiesLaws W G → derivedSeries G d = ⊥) ∧
      ∃ w : FreeGroup ℕ, (∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G], SatisfiesLaws W G →
        SatisfiesLaws {w} G) ∧ ¬ LawConsequence W w

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 5.43 "Yes, there does", citing Kleiman (Trans. Moscow
Math. Soc. 1983, no. 2, 63-110). Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems
in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved
Problems, Problem 5.43 (A. Yu. Olshanskii); resolution: Yu. G. Kleiman, Trans. Moscow Math. Soc.
1983, no. 2, 63-110, Kourovka Archive, Problem 5.43: Yes, there does (Kleiman),
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted existsSolubleVarietyNotGeneratedByFinite_holds :
    existsSolubleVarietyNotGeneratedByFinite

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.SolubleVarietyNotFinitelyGeneratedKourovka5Problem43Wanted
